        <mxCell id="notes" value="Assumptions / open points" style="rounded=1;arcSize=4;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#666666;verticalAlign=top;align=left;spacingLeft=10;fontStyle=1;fontSize=12;" parent="layer-notes" vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry x="220" y="1820" width="760" height="760" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="notes-text" value="1. Active-Active across three data centres is SDAIA's stated target; the HA/DR design and RPO/RTO are pending. DB2 on RHEL VMs uses HADR + Pacemaker between DC1 and DC2, with an independent DR copy at DC3 (see the DB2 HA / DR page). HADR roles, the DR mechanism and RPO / RTO are to be confirmed.&lt;br&gt;2. Recommended baseline: one independent OpenShift cluster per data centre, each with its own internal ODF / Ceph storage (DC1, DC2 and Rakeezah / DC3). The ODF clusters are independent storage systems and separate failure domains; no stretched shared storage is used. DR between them uses the supported ODF Disaster Recovery architecture (ODF Regional-DR, asynchronous), not manual synchronisation of Ceph clusters. Regional-DR runs from Riyadh to Rakeezah (asynchronous; DC3 is a separate regional failure domain). RHACM manages the independent clusters and the DR relationships; it is the management / control plane and does not perform storage replication. ACM hub active at Rakeezah (neutral to the Riyadh pair), passive at ASAS-2, subject to final Red Hat validation.&lt;br&gt;3. To confirm with Red Hat / IBM: the supported ODF DR design between DC1 and DC2 and from Riyadh to Rakeezah, and whether both can be configured together; hub recovery support; workload protection policy. SDAIA will provide the ODF Advanced and ACM subscriptions, needed on both sides of each DR pair. ODF 4.22 needs OpenShift 4.22 or later.&lt;br&gt;4. Synchronous Metro-DR / Zero-RPO requirement — technology and supportability to be validated with Red Hat / IBM. Do not assume ODF Regional-DR provides synchronous replication: it is asynchronous. If zero-RPO DC1 ↔ DC2 is mandatory, validate the currently supported Metro-DR / storage architecture before finalising the design. To measure: DC1 ↔ DC2 RTT and Riyadh ↔ Rakeezah latency, bandwidth and replication capacity.&lt;br&gt;5. ODF DR protects OpenShift persistent volumes and application data. Db2 running on RHEL VMs uses its own HA/DR mechanism and is not connected to ODF DR. Kafka DR is MirrorMaker 2 to Rakeezah, per the Data Architect's slide; Kafka HA across ASAS-1 and ASAS-2 is not stated. If the Data Fabric / Lakehouse runs on OpenShift (Cloud Pak for Data), ODF DR would apply to that cluster — to confirm.&lt;br&gt;6. ACS (Advanced Cluster Security, part of Platform Plus): placement and ownership to be agreed with the security workstream.&lt;br&gt;7. Remaining DB2 for z/OS per data centre is shown read-only via CDC; the extent of data that stays on the mainframe is to be confirmed.&lt;br&gt;8. PDS (Microsoft SQL) is today a read-only replica of mainframe DB2; its TO-BE role (retain, rework or replace by Data Fabric) is open. The Data Architect's slide calls it PDS / BDS and shows it as a Kafka Connect CDC source.&lt;br&gt;9. Allocation of tables to DB2 instances awaits the table-dependency analysis; the final role of Virtual DB2 is open. The mainframe DB2 data-movement path is shown as IBM IDR log-based CDC to Kafka, per the Data Architect's slide; Data Gate is not shown and the choice is still an SDAIA decision.&lt;br&gt;10. API gateways: hybrid model per the As-Is finding (3 physical DataPower appliances, 1 per data centre, for the highest-volume paths such as Tawakkalna; containerised DataPower / CP4I for other workloads); shown generically, placement not drawn. Data Fabric is one logical layer; its deployment per data centre is to be confirmed.&lt;br&gt;11. Not shown: P-cloud (later phase) and mainframe workloads other than DB2 (e.g. SADAD). Security beyond WAF/LB, API Gateway and IAM belongs to a separate workstream.&lt;br&gt;12. Observability tools follow the IBM findings report; connector protocols and ports are not in the sources.&lt;br&gt;13. The Projects Scope mentions 8 OpenShift Platform Plus clusters with no breakdown; this diagram shows one per data centre plus the Data Fabric data-tier cluster(s), whose placement is open.&lt;br&gt;14. The Data Fabric strip (Ingestion → Event backbone → Consumers) follows the Data Architect's Target State slide and is not yet confirmed elsewhere. To confirm: Benefits &amp; Policy app (not a pilot application); the slide's 65 SQL Server servers versus the three PDS instances shown; IDR versus Kafka Connect for Db2 LUW; the dashboard tool.&lt;br&gt;15. External access is only through governed APIs, with no direct database access, per the Data Architect's slide. The Lakehouse federation over DB2 LUW per data centre is not drawn. The slide's gateway layer is shown as one label on the DMZ API gateway; the G2G and Entity gateways are not drawn separately.&lt;br&gt;16. Independent ODF per OpenShift cluster removes the common stretched-storage failure domain and allows each data centre to maintain its own local persistent-storage infrastructure.&lt;br&gt;17. Summary: DC1 and DC2 operate as independent OpenShift environments with independent local ODF storage. Application / data protection between the sites is provided through a supported ODF DR architecture rather than a shared stretched storage platform. Rakeezah provides regional DR. Db2 VM HA/DR remains a separate architecture. If SDAIA requires zero-RPO synchronous protection between DC1 and DC2, the Metro-DR technology must be separately validated with Red Hat / IBM before being selected.&lt;br&gt;18. User traffic path: User → GSLB / DNS → F5 / API gateway → OCP cluster → application; DC1 and DC2 can serve production traffic per the application Active-Active design (to confirm). ACM, ODF replication and Ramen are not in the user request path. User traffic routing and storage / DR replication are separate architectural planes." style="text;html=1;whiteSpace=wrap;align=left;verticalAlign=top;fontSize=11;spacingLeft=4;" parent="notes" vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry x="10" y="30" width="740" height="720" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
